---
## User question:
How to configure Nokia routers to allow LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, including those that can exit AS 65530?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure Nokia routers to allow LLGR (Long-lived Graceful Restart) stale routes to be advertised to all peers, including those that can exit AS 65530, you need to enable graceful restart and related features. Here are the CLI commands:

```
configure router bgp
    graceful-restart
    enable-notification
exit
```

If you need to apply this to specific groups or neighbors (for example, to peers exiting AS 65530):

```
configure router bgp group <group-name>
    graceful-restart
    enable-notification
exit
```

Or for individual neighbors:
```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address>
    graceful-restart
    enable-notification
exit
```

These commands enable the router to participate in graceful restart procedures and advertise stale routes to all peers, including those in different ASes.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors [ without-no-export | no without-no-export ]
no advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors
FuncDef:
This command allows BGP routes marked as LLGR stale to be advertised to BGP peers that did not advertise the LLGR capability when the session was opened. Thenoversion of this command causes advertisement behavior to follow the rule that stale routes cannot be advertised to a peer that does not understand or implement the LLGR capability. Stale routes are withdrawn towards such peers.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'without-no-export', 'Info': 'Allows LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, such that they can exit the local AS.'}
