To configure Nokia routers for VPN route exchange between different autonomous systems under BGP, use the following CLI commands:

```plaintext
configure router
    bgp
        # Configure BGP router ID (use system IPv4 address)
        router-id <ip-address>

        # Enable extended next-hop encoding for VPN routes
        extended-nh-encoding vpn-ipv4

        # Allow EBGP and IBGP routes to be treated equally for best-path selection
        best-path-selection
            ebgp-ibgp-equal vpn-ipv4
        exit

        # Configure BGP group for the peer in different AS
        group <group-name>
            type external
            peer-as <remote-as-number>
            
            # Enable next-hop-self for VPN routes
            next-hop-self
            
            # Disable capability negotiation if needed
            no disable-capability-negotiation
            
            # Configure prefix limits for VPN routes
            prefix-limit vpn-ipv4 <limit> threshold <percentage>
        exit

        # Configure neighbor under the group
        group <group-name>
            neighbor <peer-ip-address>
                # Enable third-party next-hop if peers are in same subnet
                third-party-nexthop
                
                # Enable rapid withdrawal of routes
                rapid-withdrawal
            exit
        exit
        
        # Configure route leaking between routing instances if needed
        rib-management
            vpn-ipv4
                leak-import "<policy-name>"
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

Key elements in this configuration:
1. `extended-nh-encoding` enables proper next-hop handling for VPN routes
2. `ebgp-ibgp-equal` allows equal treatment of routes from different ASes
3. `next-hop-self` ensures proper next-hop propagation
4. `prefix-limit` protects against excessive route advertisements
5. `leak-import` enables controlled route exchange between routing instances
