You are an expert assistant in configuring network devices. Based on the user's request and the retrieved content from the router configuration manual below, generate appropriate CLI configuration commands.

---

User Request:  
How to configure a Nokia router to enable BGP resolution of next-hops for labeled routes using IGP route table lookups when acting as a Route Reflector without tunnel signaling protocol participation?

Retrieved Reference Content (from the configuration manual):  
{
    "PageTitle": "aigp",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables or disables Accumulated IGP (AIGP) path attribute support with one or more BGP peers. BGP path selection among routes with an associated AIGP metric is based on the end-to-end IGP metrics of the different BGP paths, even when these BGP paths span more than one AS and IGP instance.",
    "CLIs": [
        "aigp",
        "no aigp"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no aigp"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "primary-ip-address",
    "FuncDef": "This command specifies the primary IP address of a reference location used for BGP optimal route reflection. Up to three IPv4 addresses and three IPv6 addresses can be specified per location.",
    "CLIs": [
        "primary-ip-address <ipv4-address>",
        "no primary-ip-address"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>orr>location"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "ipv4-address",
            "Info": "Specifies the primary IPv4 address of a location expressed in dotted decimal notation. Values-a.b.c.d."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no primary-ip-address"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "import",
    "FuncDef": "This command specifies route policies that control the handling of inbound routes received from certain peers. Route policies are configured in theconfig>router>policy-optionscontext.",
    "CLIs": [
        "import <plcy-or-long-expr> [ <plcy-or-expr> [ <plcy-or-expr> ] ]",
        "no import"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "plcy-or-long-expr",
            "Info": "Specifies the route policy name (up to 64 characters long) or a policy logical expression (up to 255 characters long). Allowed values are any string up to 255 characters long composed of printable, 7-bit ASCII characters. If the string contains special characters (#, $, spaces, and so on), the entire string must be enclosed within double quotes."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "plcy-or-expr",
            "Info": "Specifies the route policy name (up to 64 characters long) or a policy logical expression (up to 64 characters long). Allowed values are any string up to 64 characters long composed of printable, 7-bit ASCII characters. If the string contains special characters (#, $, spaces, and so on), the entire string must be enclosed within double quotes."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no import"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors",
    "FuncDef": "This command allows BGP routes marked as LLGR stale to be advertised to BGP peers that did not advertise the LLGR capability when the session was opened. Thenoversion of this command causes advertisement behavior to follow the rule that stale routes cannot be advertised to a peer that does not understand or implement the LLGR capability. Stale routes are withdrawn towards such peers.",
    "CLIs": [
        "advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors [ without-no-export | no without-no-export ]",
        "no advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>graceful-restart>long-lived",
        "config>router>bgp>group>graceful-restart>long-lived",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor>graceful-restart>long-lived"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "without-no-export",
            "Info": "Allows LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, such that they can exit the local AS."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "next-hop-self",
    "FuncDef": "This command configures BGP to advertise routes to members of a group or to a specific neighbor using a local address of the BGP instance as the BGP next-hop address. Note thatnext-hop-selfis set without exception, regardless of the route source (EBGP or IBGP) or its family. When used with VPN-IPv4 and VPN-IPv6 routes theenable-rr-vpn-forwardingcommand should also be configured.",
    "CLIs": [
        "next-hop-self",
        "no next-hop-self"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no next-hop-self"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "third-party-nexthop",
    "FuncDef": "Use this command to enable the router to send third-party next-hop to EBGP peers in the same subnet as the source peer, as described in RFC 4271. If enabled when an IPv4 or IPv6 route is received from one EBGP peer and advertised to another EBGP peer in the same IP subnet, the BGP next-hop is left unchanged. Third-party next-hop is not done if the address family of the transport does not match the address family of the route.",
    "CLIs": [
        "third-party-nexthop",
        "no third-party-nexthop"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no third-party-nexthop"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "enable-peer-tracking",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables BGP peer tracking. BGP peer tracking allows a BGP peer to be dropped immediately if the route used to resolve the BGP peer address is removed from the IP routing table and there is no alternative available. The BGP peer will not wait for the holdtimer to expire; therefore, the BGP re-convergence process is accelerated.",
    "CLIs": [
        "enable-peer-tracking",
        "no enable-peer-tracking"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no enable-peer-tracking"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "leak-import",
    "FuncDef": "This command is used to specify route policies that control the importation of leak-eligible routes from the BGP RIB of another routing instance into the unlabeled-IPv4, unlabeled-IPv6, or labeled-IPv4 RIB of the base router. To leak a route from one routing instance to another, the origin and destination RIB types must be the same; for example, it is not possible to leak a route from an unlabeled-IPv4 RIB of a VPRN into the labeled-IPv4 RIB of the base router.",
    "CLIs": [
        "leak-import <plcy-or-long-expr> [ <plcy-or-expr> ]",
        "no leak-import"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>rib-management>ipv4",
        "config>router>bgp>rib-management>label-ipv4",
        "config>router>bgp>rib-management>ipv6"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "plcy-or-long-expr",
            "Info": "Specifies up to 14 route policy names (up to 64 characters long) or a policy logical expression (up to 255 characters long). Allowed values are any string up to 255 characters long composed of printable, 7-bit ASCII characters. If the string contains special characters (#, $, spaces, and so on), the entire string must be enclosed within double quotes."
        },
        {
            "Parameters": "plcy-or-expr",
            "Info": "The route policy name (up to 64 characters long) or a policy logical expression (up to 64 characters long). Allowed values are any string up to 64 characters long composed of printable, 7-bit ASCII characters. If the string contains special characters (#, $, spaces, and so on), the entire string must be enclosed within double quotes."
        }
    ],
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no leak-import"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "ebgp-ibgp-equal",
    "FuncDef": "This command instructs the BGP decision process to ignore the difference between EBGP and IBGP routes in selecting the best path and eligible multipaths (if multipath and ECMP are enabled). The result is a form of EIBGP load-balancing in a multipath scenario.",
    "CLIs": [
        "ebgp-ibgp-equal [ ipv4 ] [ ipv6 ] [ label-ipv4 ] [ label-ipv6 ] [ vpn-ipv4 ] [ vpn-ipv6 ]",
        "no ebgp-ibgp-equal"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp>best-path-selection"
    ],
    "ParaDef": [
        {
            "Parameters": "ipv4",
