As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
How to configure a Nokia router to preserve the original BGP next-hop when advertising routes between EBGP peers within the same subnet?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure a Nokia router to preserve the original BGP next-hop when advertising routes between EBGP peers within the same subnet, you need to disable the `next-hop-self` feature for the relevant BGP group or neighbor. Here's the CLI command:

```
configure router bgp group <group-name> no next-hop-self
```

or for a specific neighbor:

```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address> no next-hop-self
```

Replace `<group-name>` and `<ip-address>` with your actual BGP group name and neighbor IP address. This ensures the original BGP next-hop is preserved when advertising routes to EBGP peers in the same subnet.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
third-party-nexthop
no third-party-nexthop
FuncDef:
Use this command to enable the router to send third-party next-hop to EBGP peers in the same subnet as the source peer, as described in RFC 4271. If enabled when an IPv4 or IPv6 route is received from one EBGP peer and advertised to another EBGP peer in the same IP subnet, the BGP next-hop is left unchanged. Third-party next-hop is not done if the address family of the transport does not match the address family of the route.
ParaDef:
No ParaDef found
